      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (20%):</strong> Both sides maintain current operational tempos without an acute new escalation event or ceasefire, settling into a contested baseline that persists through August while the medium-term F11 framework path (p=0.44) remains conditionally live.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (15%):</strong> A ceasefire emerges faster than current public signals suggest (<span style="color:var(--green)">F8: p=0.13</span> near-term floor), enabling the medium-term F11 framework path and beginning Hormuz traffic recovery toward the F10 threshold — requiring a back-channel breakthrough not visible in open-source signals or an Iranian domestic crisis forcing a posture change.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iran's state media entered August 2026 carrying IRGC-aligned framing that "survival constitutes victory." Outlets affiliated with the IRGC have made control of the Strait of Hormuz — not battlefield performance, territorial integrity, or nuclear capability — the central narrative pillar. BG Javani (IRGC Deputy Commander Political Affairs) declared the US was "desperately trying to reverse Iran's strategic victory in the Strait." Moqavemat-linked channels reframe infrastructure collapse — power outages, fuel rationing, dam depletion — as a temporary price for a permanent geopolitical achievement. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Mojtaba Khamenei's five-month absence from any confirmed in-person public appearance created a communicative vacuum that state media filled through secondary instruments: IRGC commanders, designated spokespersons, and attributed written statements. A July 19 written statement attributed to Khamenei called for "sacred unity amid political divides" — broadcast on state media as a consolidation message. IRGC Commander Abdollahi on July 24 stated the IRGC would "kill an American servicemember for every Iranian killed," broadcast prime-time on IRIB. A physically absent Supreme Leader alongside an operationally visible IRGC commander as the public face of the regime is without precedent in the Islamic Republic's history. → supports <span class="cite">F12</span></p>
      <p>Iran's domestic communication in July followed a pattern of selective information management. The July 30 announcement of a 30% gasoline quota cut was aired at 11pm on minor state channels; IRIB led that same night with footage of IRGC missile launches. Iran International and NCRI documented coordinated resistance unit activity across multiple cities through the third week of July. State media carried no acknowledgment of resistance activity. Power outages across 18 of 31 provinces and dam levels at century lows create material conditions that the "strategic victory" operational framing cannot fully offset. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

